.TH reboot 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
shutdown, halt, reboot \- stop or restart the machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shutdown
.br
.B halt
[\fB\-f\fR]
.br
.B reboot
[\fB\-f\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
One binary under three names.
.TP
.B shutdown
The polite one.  It asks
.B init
for run level 6, which runs
.BR /etc/rc.halt :
processes are killed, filesystems are unmounted, and the machine is left
in a state the card can be pulled out of.  This is the one to use.
.TP
.B reboot
Asks
.B init
for run level 5, and restarts the machine if
.B init
does not answer.
.TP
.B halt
Stops the machine now, without asking
.BR init .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Do not sync first.  Skips the tidy path entirely and goes straight to
the kernel.  Everything not yet written is lost, and the next boot will
run
.BR fsck .
There is very little reason to use this.
.SH NOTES
Only the superuser may stop the machine.
.PP
On the Pico Computer the machine does not power off; a halt leaves it
stopped, and the reset button or a power cycle starts it again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR telinit (1),
.BR sync (1),
.BR remount (1),
.BR fsck (1)
